s.\n\n**Cause.** `to_cents` ran `int(float(amount) * 100)`. Most decimal prices have no exact binary float value. So `float(\"19.99\") * 100` gives `1998.9999999999998`. Then `int()` cuts off the fraction, which leaves `1998`. Each wrong price threw off the invoice totals that used it.\n\n| Input | Expected cents | Old result |\n|---|---|---|\n| `\"19.99\"` | 1999 | 1998 |\n| `\"0.29\"` | 29 | 28 |\n\n**Fix.** `to_cents` now does the maths in `Decimal`, which is exact. It rounds half-up to a whole cent, so input with more than 2 decimal places still rounds correctly:\n\n```python\ncents = Decimal(str(amount)) * 100\nreturn int(cents.quantize(Decimal(\"1\"), rounding=ROUND_HALF_UP))\n```\n\n**Not changed.** `invoicing/tax.py` has a similar risk. It works out tax with float rates and Python's `round()`. That function rounds an exact half-cent to the even number rather than up. The tests don't hit this case, but some totals could be off by one cent. Moving tax to `Decimal` with half-up rounding would fix it. I can make that change if you want it.","ttft_ms":5196,"type":"result","duration_ms":19498,"uuid":"46fc8718-83fd-4d47-96d6-b2fbe5916fd7","ttft_stream_ms":4484,"time_to_request_ms":148,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":11,"system_prompt":9,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":1,"input_attachments":7,"input_hooks":1,"tool_pool_refresh":1,"init_message":2,"engine_pickup":1,"message_prep":0,"model_config_wait":1,"autocompact":0,"query_setup":0,"model_gates":98,"tool_schema_build":11,"message_normalization":0,"client_creation":4,"request_build":1},"turn_start_resume_kind":"none","process_turn_index":0,"time_to_request_loop_lag_ms":98,"time_to_request_cpu_ms":308,"time_to_request_major_faults":0,"input_attachments_detail":{"slowest_producer":"model","slowest_producer_ms":5,"cpu_ms":13,"major_faults":0},"first_content_frame_ms":4484,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
